  "query_preview": "/Friday through Tuesday/...\nIssued 234 PM MST TUE NOV 10 2026\nA deep cut off low pressure system will be moving onshore and east \nof the area through the weekend. Strong south/southwesterly flow \naloft will increase during the day Friday as the system moves into \nthe Pacific NW, with wind gusts 35-50 mph across the open terrain of \nsoutheast Oregon and far southwest Idaho, and 20-40 mph elsewhere. \nHigh temperatures on Friday will remain near record highs, warmest \nacross the lower valleys of southwest Idaho, as the system moves in. \nThe trough will weaken and become more of an open wave Friday into \nSaturday as it moves over the area, with a cold front moving west-to-\neast as well. Precipitation chances will rise to 50-90% Friday night \nthrough Saturday morning, highest across the mountains. \nPrecipitation chances will gradually taper off through the day \nSaturday. Snow levels will begin the event 7-8 kft MSL and lower to \n6 kft MSL by the end of the event. Winds will remain gusty on \nSaturday, but not as strong as Friday. Highs Saturday will cool 5-10 \ndegrees from Friday.\n\nFollowing this systems exit early Sunday, upper-level ridging will \nbring a brief dry period before the next system begins to move in. \nAnother deep cutoff low moving inland from the Gulf of Alaska will \nbe the weather maker early next week, bringing a 40-60% chance of \nprecipitation to the area. Previous forecast models had this system \nmoving in Sunday but recent trends and agreement have it now on \nMonday and Tuesday. Winds will weaken but continue to be breezy as \nthe trough moves in. Temperatures will remain similar on Sunday, \nthen cool several more degrees on Monday as colder air arrives with \nthe trough. Snow levels are also forecast to lower to 4-6 kft MSL by \nTuesday. Precipitation chances trend lower on Tuesday, but troughing \noverhead will keep conditions unsettled with temperatures cooling a \nfew more degrees.",
